 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Multi-Dimensional Transfer Function Memory Layout](5649-multi-dimensional-transfer-function-memory-layout.md)
-This method defines the memory layout and storage overhead for hardware-based volume rendering using multi-dimensional transfer functions (MTFs). It addresses the problem that hardware renderers cannot compute transfer function variables on the fly, requiring all variables to be stored at each voxel sample point. It is distinguished from standard scalar volume rendering by the explicit inclusion of a quantized normal volume to support surface-based classification widgets, which imposes specific alignment constraints on the texture memory layout.
+Extends this layout to include explicit normal volumes for surface-based widgets, imposing stricter alignment constraints.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Memory Complexity:** The memory footprint is determined by the product of the quantized dimensions of the transfer function variables. For a standard 3D layout, this is $256 \times 128 \times 8$ texels [S1, book].
-*   **Expressivity vs. Performance:** The separable layout ($256^2 + 256$) offers reduced memory usage but limits expressivity by preventing the specification of unique combinations of all three data values [S1, book].
-*   **Hardware Limitations:** Strict alignment requirements (1, 2, or 4 values per texel) can lead to wasted memory if the number of required values is 3 [S1, book].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** $O(N_d \times N_g \times N_s)$ for the full 3D texture. For separable layouts, $O(N_d \times N_g + N_s)$.
+*   **Performance:** Hardware texture sampling provides $O(1)$ lookup time per ray step. The separable approach reduces memory bandwidth requirements for the transfer function lookup.
+*   **Strengths:** Efficient hardware acceleration; supports complex multi-dimensional classification.
+*   **Limitations:** Fixed resolution limits the granularity of classification; memory alignment restrictions force padding (e.g., 3 values $\to$ 4 texels); separable layouts reduce classification expressivity [S1, book].
 
